                  %a{:href => "#ReferencesRelationBetweenAQFTAndFQFT"} Relation between algebraic and functorial field theory
          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            %strong Algebraic Quantum Field Theory
            or
            %strong Axiomatic Quantum Field Theory
            or
            %strong AQFT
            for short is a formalization of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+field+theory"} quantum field theory
            (and specifically full, hence
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/non-perturbative+quantum+field+theory"} non-perturbative quantum field theory
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebras+of+observables"} algebras of observables
            to patches of parameter space (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spacetime"}> spacetime
            ,
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/worldvolume"} worldvolume
          %p
            As such, the approach of AQFT is roughly dual to that of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/FQFT"} FQFT
            %em spaces of states
            are assigned to boundaries of
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cobordism"} cobordism
          %p
            One may roughly think of AQFT as being a formalization of what in basic
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+mechanics"} quantum mechanics
            textbooks is called the
            %strong
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Heisenberg+picture"} Heisenberg picture
            of quantum mechanics. On the other hand
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/FQFT"} FQFT
            axiomatizes the
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Schr%C3%B6dinger+picture"} Schrödinger picture
            \.
          %p
            The axioms of traditional AQFT encode the properties of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/local+net"} local net
            of observables and are called the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Haag-Kastler+axioms"} Haag-Kastler axioms
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+field+theory"} quantum field theory
            on a solid conceptual footing.
          %p
            From the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/nPOV"} nPOV
            we may think of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/local+net"} local net
            as a co-flabby
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/presheaf"} copresheaf
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebra"} algebras
            on spacetime which satisfies a certain
            %em locality
            axiom with respect to the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+Lorentzian+manifold"} Lorentzian structure
            of
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spacetime"} spacetime
          %ul
            %li
              %strong locality:
              algebras assigned to spacelike separated regions commute with each other when embedded into any joint superalgebra.
          %p
            This is traditionally formulated (implicitly) as a structure in ordinary
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+theory"} category theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cobordism+hypothesis"} cobordism hypothesis
            and the corresponding
            = succeed "-formulation" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2Cn%29-category"} (∞,n)-category
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/FQFT"} FQFT
            also
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+category+theory"} higher categorical
            versions of systems of local algebras of observables are being put forward and studied. Three structures are curently being studied, that are all conceptually very similar and similar to the Haag-Kastler axioms:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/factorization+algebra"}> factorization algebra
                s
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+chiral+homology"} topological chiral homology
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/blob+homology"}> blob homology
                \.
          %p
            Initially, all three of these encoded what in physics are called
            %em Euclidean
            quantum field theories, whereas only the notion of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/local+net"} local net
            incorporated the fact that the underlying spacetime of a quantum field theory is a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+Lorentzian+space"} smooth Lorentzian space
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/factorization+algebra"} factorization algebra
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lorentzian+manifolds"} Lorentzian manifolds
          %p
            In the context of the Haag-Kastler axioms there is a precise theorem, the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Osterwalder-Schrader+theorem"} Osterwalder-Schrader theorem
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Wick+rotation"} Wick rotation
          %p Sheaves are used explicitly in:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                Roberts, John E.:
                %a{:href => "http://books.google.com/books?id=IFjzuLjE43kC&lpg=PA297&ots=5Ld1B3I45m&dq=Operator%20algebras%20and%20applications%2C%20Part%202&pg=PA523#v=onepage&q=Operator%20algebras%20and%20applications,%20Part%202&f=false"} New light on the mathematical structure of algebraic field theory.
                Operator algebras and applications, Part 2 (Kingston, Ont., 1980), pp. 523–550, Proc. Sympos. Pure Math., 38, Amer. Math. Soc., Providence, R.I., 1982.
            %li
              %p
                Roberts, John E.:
                = succeed "." do
                  %a{:href => "https://projecteuclid.org/journals/communications-in-mathematical-physics/volume-85/issue-1/Localization-in-algebraic-field-theory/cmp/1103921341.full"} Localization in algebraic field theory
          %p — much information to be filled in —
          %h2#axioms Axioms
          %ul
            %li
